# valgrind suppressions for the test nginx (docs/test/README.md, "valgrind in
# CI"). Each entry is an allocation of nginx itself that valgrind reports as
# a leak when the test nginx exits, with the reason why it is not
# ngx_mruby's. Leaks with a frame of ngx_mruby, mruby or a gem are never
# suppressed here.

# nginx.c, ngx_set_environment(): the environment array that the process
# runs with (environ). Its pool cleanup, ngx_cleanup_environment(), leaves
# the array allocated while environ still points to it, "as it happens on
# exit, the only option is to leak it" (the comment in nginx.c). At exit,
# valgrind runs glibc's __libc_freeres, whose free_mem in stdlib/setenv.c
# calls clearenv(), which sets environ to NULL; no pointer to the array is
# left, so valgrind reports it as definitely lost, not still reachable:
# 8 bytes in 1 block in every cell of the build matrix (nginx 1.26.3 to
# 1.31.6), with the stack
# malloc / ngx_alloc / ngx_set_environment (nginx.c:613 in 1.31.6) /
# ngx_single_process_cycle / main.
{
   nginx-environment-at-exit
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite
   fun:malloc
   fun:ngx_alloc
   fun:ngx_set_environment
   ...
}

# ngx_event_process_init() in a worker process (master_process on, the
# nginx of test/t/cases/_workers.rb): cycle->connections, cycle->read_events
# and cycle->write_events, which nginx allocates with ngx_alloc and never
# frees. A worker that ends on SIGTERM (ngx_worker_process_exit()) destroys
# the cycle pool, which holds the pointers to the three arrays, and calls
# exit(); the arrays point only at each other (c->read, c->write,
# ev->data). Whether a stale pointer to the connections array is left
# depends on the platform: on the x86-64 runner valgrind reports, for each
# such worker, 14,848 bytes in 1 block definitely lost (the connections,
# ngx_event.c:751 in 1.31.8) and 6,144 bytes in each of 2 blocks indirectly
# lost (the events, ngx_event.c:758 and 770; run 41226606511 of #706); in
# the arm64 container the same blocks are possibly lost. test/lsan.supp
# suppresses the same arrays for LeakSanitizer. The entry needs the frame
# ngx_worker_process_init, so the single process of the test nginx, whose
# arrays are possibly lost at exit ("valgrind in CI" in
# docs/test/README.md), is not covered.
{
   nginx-worker-event-arrays-at-exit
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect
   fun:malloc
   fun:ngx_alloc
   fun:ngx_event_process_init
   fun:ngx_worker_process_init
   ...
}
